{"chunk_id": "3509ed216afb", "text": "Arjuna, overwhelmed by compassion on the battlefield, argues that killing the Kauravas\u2014his own kin and teachers\u2014would destroy family traditions, corrupt society, and condemn both victors and victims to sin. He declares he would rather die unarmed than kill them and collapses in grief. Krishna rebukes this despair as unworthy of a warrior and begins teaching him. He explains the immortality of the soul: bodies perish, but the self is eternal and cannot be harmed. Therefore mourning the inevitable cycle of birth and death is misguided. Krishna also introduces the foundations of Karma\u2011yoga: perform one\u2019s duty without attachment to results, remain steady in success or failure, and discipline the senses. True wisdom lies in equanimity, self\u2011control, and devotion to right action rather than pursuit of pleasure or reward.\nArjuna refuses to fight; Krishna rebukes his despair and teaches the immortality of the soul, duty without attachment, and the marks of steady wisdom.\nThemes: duty vs compassion, immortality of the soul, detachment from results, yoga and equanimity, self-mastery", "vector": [-0.004536427091807127, -0.017720313742756844, -0.006108390633016825, 0.01845145970582962, -0.005024965386837721, 0.01431716326624155, -0.022386353462934494, -0.006384231615811586, -0.03033590316772461, -0.014489979483187199, 0.013200504705309868, 0.018504634499549866, 0.013579370453953743, 0.0035360867623239756, -0.0023230495862662792, -0.0030608419328927994, 0.05336034670472145, -0.017653847113251686, 0.03490888699889183, -0.01617826148867607, -0.0025174678303301334, 0.0049352338537573814, 0.00017655508418101817, -0.009910348802804947, -0.00984388031065464, 0.032276760786771774, 0.002554025035351515, -0.03376564010977745, 0.01369236595928669, -0.03262239322066307, -0.00903297308832407, -0.001435704412870109, -0.0012446094769984484, -0.013393260538578033, -0.04004019871354103, 0.00959794968366623, 0.0022997858468443155, 